## Continuation update (2026-09-25T04:52Z)

- Continued the Java 5.5.1 remoting comparison. Added configured high/low outbound byte-watermark hysteresis and a focused queue-drain test; the setting measures Rust queued bytes and is explicitly not Netty event-loop writability. Applied socket send/receive options to both direct TCP and SOCKS5 sockets via `socket2`; enabled pooled allocator is rejected rather than silently ignored. Java watermarks accept high-only configuration (low defaults to zero), which Rust validation now allows.
- NameServer invoke now tries another configured server after connection establishment failures while preserving one deadline; once a connection succeeds, a remote invoke failure is returned without replaying the command. Added a scripted failover test. This is offline loopback, not Broker evidence.
- Updated compatibility matrix, README and ADR to clarify implemented controls and remaining mismatches. Phase 3 parity remains unaccepted: Java callback/send error taxonomy and executor lifecycle, event overload/lifecycle, exact NameServer chosen-address/update semantics, TLS property/config-file/reload controls, pooled allocator and worker-group controls, and broader overload/error behavior remain. No plan edits, commit, Phase 4 branch, live Broker or Docker validation.
